{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>If he will hate us, etc.</b> — meaning, perhaps (דלמא); but this word has no parallel in the entire Torah, and one needs to know why Scripture should employ an expression whose ordinary sense is the opposite of the intended meaning. And even though there is no room to err and understand it as anything other than 'perhaps,' nevertheless it should have used a proper term such as 'פן' or 'אולי.'" "And it appears that Scripture is speaking its own words with 'לוּ' — and the intention in this is that they feared concerning something for which, would that it were so, he would indeed respond to them — namely, His saying <b>he will requite us all the evil</b>. And the tribes would suffer in measure exactly as Yosef suffered from their side. And through this, in the end they would not be liable to the exiles and the tribulations for this sin, as they of blessed memory said (Shabbat 10a) [regarding] the Egyptian exile and also in the final exile. Go out and learn what happened to the ten pillars of the world:"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 50, :notes {0 "The verse reads לוּ יִשׂטְמֵנוּ, and Or HaChaim addresses why לוּ (normally 'if only' or 'would that') is used where 'perhaps/lest' is meant.", 1 "The citation '(שבת י:)' is resolved as Shabbat 10a based on the well-known statement there that the exile was decreed for the sale of Yosef."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:51:21.737486833Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}